>> * notes/log file
> 
> Is this an automatically created log?

Yes. It tracks things like outstanding achievements (in Nethack, this would
be getting the Candelabrum, the Quest artefact, getting crowned etc). You
can configure to note risky situations, a la (made up for Nethack)
turn 1509  D:10  3/37 hp (a dwarf king)
turn 4954  D:15  started to turn to stone (a cockatrice),
it can note down skills gained and spells learned, particularly dangerous
monsters slain and so on.

>> * configurable character dumps
> 
> Just a post game feature.

Not only that, there's more to it. All roguelikes seem to have this by now.
You can produce a dump during play, to read it yourself or post somewhere,
asking for help. It contains your inventory, the intrinsics (only as far as
you are aware of them if dumping while playing), above notes, a plan of the
dungeon (where are shops, the mines, etc.), a list of killed monsters and
some more.

>> * tutorial
> 
> Built-in?

Yes, and trying to be intelligent. There are three mainline combinations
(melee fighter, pure ranger, pure caster - Crawl has very many class/race
choices, so this helps beginners focus on the simple stuff). These explain
the interface and react on certain happenings (you getting poisoned etc.)
This was met with very good feedback. Essentially, you can log on one of
the telnet servers, start the tutorial and never needed to read a line of
the documentation.

>> * macros
> 
> Hmm.. - I doubt to call that a desirable feature in a type of game that
> I'd wish to *not* be automatically playable. (But again I have to admit
> that I know not enough about the Crawl feature to judge.)

Yes, this is to a certain extent true. Note that macros include keymappings,
which are useful in any keys (spoken by someone forced to use a German
layout :) 
 
Note that the most two important abilities are to seach for a string (like
"plate mail" and the game will list all places where it knew a plate mail
to be; you can press the appropriate buttons and will walk there
automatically) and auto-travel on it's own (go to my stash, please!).

I seriously would have trouble playing a large roguelike without these. I
remembering doing several trips Ludios-Mines Temple in order to BUC loot,
all manually. Could not stand that anymore, spoilt by autotravel :)

