> Is the Crawl endgame (and are the classes) very much more differentiated
> compared to Nethack?

Yes! It seems that Linley (the original author) played Nethack and Angband a
lot and tried to devise a cool game which does not fall into some peculiar
traps (probably creating traps on its own, as some would muse :). 

First of all, classes play an inferior role in Crawl: they determine your
starting position (hp, mp, starting skills and inventory, sometimes god)
and that is all. The main choice is that of race. Different races have
different aptitudes for skills (Crawl has more skills than Nethack, for
different weapon types, different magic realms, things like Armour,
Shields, Dodging, Stabbing etc.). So, for example, a Spriggan has great
aptitudes for Dodging, Stabbing, Stealth and certain kinds of magic
(meaning they become very proficient with these skill fast), but bad
aptitudes for Armour and most weapon types. This means that a Spriggan will
usually never be a combat wombat (in particular due to the lack of hp
necessary for that - there is one skill (Fighting) governing hp use, but no
other ways to improve it). Given time and dedication, you may turn your
Spriggan into something like a light-armoured swordfighter, but that would
be a bit nontrivial. There is more to races: for example, Spriggans are
very small (making body armour benefit them less) but very quick.
Next, there are almost no permanent intrinsics. Intrinsics are provided by
jewellery, randarts (so, a randart cloak could provide fire resistance), or
mutations. This means that you have to be flexible with threats - it rarely
happens that you just equip all your gear and are protected from everything
dangerous. Finally, there is no surefire means of wishing (only a scroll of
acquirement, who produces random artefacts). This means that you usually
have to make with what you find, avoiding the ascension kit phenomenon.
Several devices allow you to react flexible to strategical and/or tactical
shortcomings, including developing skills, choice of god, and choice of
branches (Crawl has widely varying optional branches).

